          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What screening tool does guidance describe for anxiety, and what does the score change?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found is from the anxiety document, \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\" (preventive services and psychiatric association guidance).\n\n**The tool:** It describes the seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7). It is the instrument most often used both to screen for anxiety and to track how severe it is.\n\n**What the score changes:** The document lists these severity bands:\n- 5 or more: mild\n- 10 or more: moderate\n- 15 or more: severe\n\nA score of 10 is the point at which further assessment usually follows.\n\n**Screening in general:** The document says screening adults for anxiety disorders makes sense where systems are in place to ensure accurate diagnosis and follow-up. The strongest statement is for adults under 65. For older adults, the document says the evidence is insufficient to make a recommendation either way. That is a statement about the evidence, not a recommendation against screening.\n\n**What this does not cover:** The retrieved passages do not give the questionnaire items or how to score them. They also do not say what treatment follows each band. The \"first-line care\" part of the title was not in the passages I received. A clinician can explain what a score means for an individual person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
